var tafsirData_69 = {
  "1": "\"The Inevitable Hour!\" opens Surah al-Haqqah with a thunderous name for the Day of Gathering. The single word announces certainty after the Reminder for the whole world and after gardens of this world reduced to ashes and gardens of Bliss promised.\n\n“The Inevitable Hour!” translates “al-Haqqah,” the True, Real, Inevitable occurrence that must happen, the same root as “haqq al-yaqin,” absolute truth, and “Blessed is the One in Whose Hands rests all authority.” Inevitable stresses that it will occur, as creation without flaw, the pen’s writing, and the command descending between seven heavens guarantee. Hour, or “sa‘ah,” in Quranic use often means the appointed time that never delays, when the Trumpet is blown with one blast and earth and mountains are crushed with one blow, when the sky is torn and eight angels bear the Throne, when you are presented and none of your secrets stay hidden.\n\nThe exclamation marks awe, as with “What is the Inevitable Hour?” next, and mirrors the garden’s sudden torment while they slept that no poor could enter today, showing inevitable can come while you sleep, and the caravan’s fanfare that distracted from the pulpit, showing inevitable can be missed while you chase merchandise.\n\nThis follows the whole world reminder to show what is reminded about most urgently. The Reminder is for the whole world because the Inevitable Hour is for the whole world, all earlier and later generations, all dispersed then gathered, all who believed then abandoned and whose hearts sealed, all who gave rightful share and walked upright.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat Inevitable Hour as distant folklore, as ancient fables dismissal did. Ancient fables charge calls it old tales; Inevitable calls it present reality already written before creation where every calamity or blessing is written.\n\nFor the listener, hearing “The Inevitable Hour!” should do what looking again at flawless heavens does, humble and make you listen and reason, so you are not among those who said “When will this threat come?” with mockery but delay, but among those in awe without seeing whose mighty reward is forgiveness, who hear the call on Friday and proceed diligently, and whose faces will not be gloomy when the Hour comes but will be among the righteous with Gardens of Bliss.",
  "2": "\"What is the Inevitable Hour?\" deepens the opening after proclaiming “The Inevitable Hour!” The question is not for information, but to magnify.\n\n“What is the Inevitable Hour?” translates “ma al-Haqqah,” what is that Inevitable? The repetition, as with “What is the Striking Disaster? And what will make you realize what the Striking Disaster is?” after Thamud and ‘Ad denied, magnifies enormity. It signals that the reality exceeds description, like the Fire nearly bursting in fury that human words can only approach, like the Garden whose provision is excellent beyond merchandise, and like the seven heavens created one above another whose flaw you will never see no matter how you look.\n\nThe question follows the exclamation to move the heart from hearing name to feeling weight. Thamud and ‘Ad denied the Striking Disaster, and this is that Disaster’s other name, the Inevitable. Their denial and destruction illustrate what the Inevitable does to deniers, yet the question shows it is greater than those illustrations alone, as with the promise that punishment of Hereafter is far worse if only they knew, and the Fire prepared for eavesdropping devils and for Withholders.\n\nThe Quran often asks “What will make you realize?” about the Inevitable to show human knowledge is limited without revelation, as with “That knowledge is with Allah alone, and I am only a clear warner” when asked when the threat will come. Realization comes not from guessing dates, but from listening to the Warner who came and not saying Allah revealed nothing.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to answer “What is it?” with speculation about dates or with ancient fables dismissal. The answer is given through the stories of Thamud’s blast, ‘Ad’s wind, Pharaoh’s crushing grip, the Ark reminder, the Trumpet, and the Throne, not through speculation.\n\nFor practice, let the question humble. You do not yet fully realize the Inevitable, but you can prepare by being among those who listened and reasoned before being among residents of Blaze, by protecting families from Fire whose fuel is people and stones, and by grasping the reminder that is for the whole world, so that when the Inevitable arrives, your realization is not gloomy-faced regret but perfected light and forgiveness.",
  "3": "\"And what will make you realize what the Inevitable Hour is?\" intensifies the awe after “What is the Inevitable Hour?” The double question marks the limits of human grasp and the need for revelation.\n\n“And what will make you realize what the Inevitable Hour is?” translates “wa ma adraka ma al-Haqqah,” and what will inform you what the Inevitable is? The phrase is used for weighty unseen realities, like the Night of Decree and the Striking Disaster, indicating that realization requires prophetic informing, not imagination, as with knowledge of when the threat will come being with Allah alone.\n\nThe question follows two prior proclamations of the Inevitable, building suspense before the answer, which is not a definition but narratives, “Thamud and ‘Ad denied the Striking Disaster...” The answer is historical tasting and future unveiling, the blast, wind, flood, Trumpet, crushing, torn sky, Throne borne by eight, and presentation where none of your secrets stay hidden. Realization comes through those images, which humble mountains and make the pen write, not through abstract debate where every cry is thought against you like planks.\n\nThis emphasizes that the Inevitable is beyond immediate experience, similar to the garden’s ashes that surprised owners who thought they lost their way, and to the provision withheld that makes you ask who will provide if He withholds. You cannot realize the Inevitable by looking at merchandise or honour that expels inferior; you realize by listening to the Warner who did come and not calling him extremely astray.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat “what will make you realize” as rhetorical with no answer, leading to indifference. The Quran answers with stories and scenes that follow, inviting reasoning.\n\nFor the living, the question is merciful. Since you cannot realize alone, accept the Informer, the Most Subtle, All-Aware who created seven heavens without flaw and knows whether you speak secretly or openly, and who sent a messenger reciting to make things clear so He may bring you from darkness to light. Realize through His telling, then act, being among those in awe without seeing who receive mighty reward, so that realizing the Inevitable now through revelation spares you from realizing it then through the Blaze that almost bursts and the gloom that covers downcast eyes.",
  "4": "“˹Both˺ Thamûd and ’Ȃd denied the Striking Disaster.” opens the account of the nations who denied the Day that the surah has been describing, and the verse names the first two of them. “Both Thamud and 'Ad denied the Striking Disaster,” or “kaththabat Thamoodu wa 'Adun bil-qari'ah.” Ibn Kathir explains that the “qari'ah” — “the Striking Disaster,” the calamity that strikes — is one of the names of the Day of Judgment, and the verse says that Thamud and 'Ad denied it, and the believer is taught that the denial of the Day of Judgment is the root of the denial of the messengers, for the one who denies the end denies the reason for the message, and the nations who denied the end denied the messenger who warned of it. Al-Tabari notes that the surah began by asking what the “Haqqah” — the Inevitable Reality — is, and now it names the nations who denied it, and the believer is taught that the denial of the Day is the old disease, and that the nations named are the witnesses of its end.\n\nAl-Razi observes the connection between the names, for the surah has called the Day by several names — the Haqqah, the Qari'ah — and the believer is taught that the many names of the Day are the many faces of its certainty, and that the nations who denied it denied the one certainty that the names were meant to fix. The verse also teaches the believer the seriousness of denying the Day, for Thamud and 'Ad were destroyed in this world because of their denial, and the believer is taught that the denial of the Day is the denial that brings the destruction, and that the one who denies the end denies the beginning of his own ruin. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to fix the Day of Judgment in his heart, for the Day is the certainty that governs the life, and the believer is taught to live as the one who is certain of the Day, and to know that the one who lives in the certainty is the one who is saved from the fate of the deniers.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to hear the names of the Day as the names of the certainty, and to know that the denial of the Day is the denial that destroyed the ancients, and the believer is taught to be of those who affirm the Day and prepare for it, and to leave the denial to those whose end is the end that the surah records.",
  "5": "“As for Thamûd, they were destroyed by an overwhelming blast.” records the destruction of Thamud, and the verse names the instrument of their ruin with a word that carries the sound of the ruin itself. “As for Thamud, they were destroyed by an overwhelming blast,” or “fa-amma Thamoodu fa-uhlikoo bit-taghiyah.” Ibn Kathir explains that the “taghiyah” — “the overwhelming blast” — is the thunderous cry, the shout that exceeded every limit, and the believer is taught that Thamud were destroyed by a single overwhelming sound, and that the punishment of God is not bound by the numbers of an army but comes in the form that He wills, and that the one blast was enough to end a people who had carved their homes into the rock. Al-Tabari notes that the word “taghiyah” is from the root of transgression and excess, and the believer is taught that the blast was the answer to their transgression, and that the punishment was fitted to the sin, the overwhelming sound answering the overwhelming defiance.\n\nAl-Razi observes the fittingness of the punishment, for Thamud had been shown the she-camel and had killed it in their defiance, and the blast that destroyed them was the blast that their defiance had called down, and the believer is taught that the sin which is committed in defiance is answered in the same measure, and that the one who transgresses the limits is met by the punishment that itself transgresses the ordinary. The verse also teaches the believer the swiftness of the divine judgment, for the blast was one, and the destruction was complete, and the believer is taught that the judgment of God does not need the time that men need, and that the one who delays his turning is gambling with the time that he cannot control. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to fear the blast that needs no army, and to know that the protection from the blast is the obedience that precedes it, and the believer is taught to make his obedience the shield that the blast cannot pass.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read the blast of Thamud as the warning of the suddenness of the judgment, and to know that the one who is certain of the Day prepares for it before the blast, and the believer is taught to prepare in the time of the warning, for the warning is the mercy that precedes the blast.",
  "6": "“And as for ’Ȃd, they were destroyed by a furious, bitter wind” records the destruction of 'Ad, and the verse names the wind that was their ruin with the same precision that named the blast of Thamud. “And as for 'Ad, they were destroyed by a furious, bitter wind,” or “wa amma 'Adun fa-uhlikoo bi-reehin sarsarin 'atiyah.” Ibn Kathir explains that the wind of 'Ad was a “sarsar” — a shrieking, bitterly cold wind — and the word “'atiyah” — “furious,” “exceeding” — describes the wind as one that surpassed every measure, and the believer is taught that the punishment of 'Ad was the punishment of the wind, the element they had taken for granted, made the instrument of their ruin, and that the one who trusts in his strength is undone by the thing he cannot seize. Al-Tabari notes that the wind was cold and violent, and the believer is taught that the wind which brings the mercy of the rain can bring the judgment of the Lord, and that the elements are the servants of their Creator, sent as He wills.\n\nAl-Razi observes the contrast between the two punishments, for Thamud were destroyed by a sound and 'Ad by a wind, and the believer is taught that the instruments of the divine punishment are many, and that the Lord who destroyed one people by the blast and another by the wind is the Lord whose armies are the elements, and that no people is safe from the judgment because the judgment may come in any form. The verse also teaches the believer the lesson of the boast, for 'Ad had said “who is mightier than we,” and the wind answered them, and the believer is taught that the boast of the mighty is the prelude to the wind, and that the one who trusts in his might is trusting in what a wind can scatter. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to fear the Lord of the wind as he hopes in the Lord of the rain, and to know that the wind that cools him is the wind that destroyed 'Ad, and the believer is taught to live in the fear and the hope together.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the wind itself as a sign, for the wind that blows through his day is the wind that is under the command of the Lord, and the believer is taught to remember 'Ad in the wind, and to know that the remembrance is the shield that the wind cannot pass.",
  "7": "“which Allah unleashed on them non-stop for seven nights and eight days, so that you would have seen its people lying dead like trunks of uprooted palm trees.” gives the measure of the wind's work, and the verse records the duration and the image of the ruin with the precision of the Quran. “Which Allah unleashed on them non-stop for seven nights and eight days, so that you would have seen its people lying dead like trunks of uprooted palm trees,” or “sakhkharaha 'alayhim sab'a layalin wa thamaniyata ayyamin husooman fa-tara al-qawma feeha sar'a ka-annahum a'jazu nakhlin khawiyah.” Ibn Kathir explains that the wind was unleashed upon 'Ad for seven nights and eight days without interruption, and the believer is taught that the punishment of 'Ad was not a passing storm but a sustained judgment, an unbroken misery that ground them down, and that the word “husooman” — “continuous,” “unrelenting” — is the measure of the punishment. Al-Tabari notes the image of the people lying like the trunks of uprooted palm trees, hollowed and emptied, and the believer is taught that the strength of 'Ad, which they had boasted of, became the image of their ruin, the mighty bodies cast down like the trunks of the palms of their own land.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the duration, for the wind of 'Ad was not the wind of a moment but the wind of seven nights and eight days, and the believer is taught that the punishment of God is not bound by the time of men, and that the one who persists in his defiance is given the punishment that persists, and that the unrelenting sin is met by the unrelenting judgment. The verse also teaches the believer the fittingness of the image, for the palm tree is the tree of 'Ad's valleys, and the comparison of the people to the trunks of their own palms makes the ruin vivid and the lesson near, and the believer is taught that the punishment is described in the imagery of the punished so that the warning may reach the heart. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to read the image of the uprooted palms as the image of every life torn from its root, and to cling to the root of faith, for the wind uproots only those whose roots are not in God.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to let the image of 'Ad remain before his eyes, for the uprooted palms are the end of the boastful, and the believer is taught to seek the rootedness that no wind can undo, the rootedness of the heart in the Lord of the wind.",
  "8": "“Do you see any of them left alive?” asks the question that turns the account of 'Ad toward its audience, and the verse summons the hearer to look for any survivor of the ruin. “Do you see any of them left alive?” or “fa-hal tara lahum min baqiyah.” Ibn Kathir explains that the question asks whether any of 'Ad remained, and the believer is taught that the destruction of 'Ad was complete, and that the question is the Quran's way of making the hearer witness the totality of the ruin, and that the one who looks for the survivor of 'Ad looks in vain, for the wind left none. Al-Tabari notes that the question is addressed to the Prophet and through him to every hearer, and the believer is taught that the absence of the survivor is the proof of the power of God, and that the nations who were mighty are now without a remnant, and that the might of the mighty is the thing that leaves no trace.\n\nAl-Razi observes the force of the question, for it places the hearer before the empty land of 'Ad and asks him to search for the living, and the believer is taught that the search is the reflection, and that the one who searches for the remnant of the ancient nations and finds none has seen the lesson with his own eyes. The verse also teaches the believer the difference between the remembrance of the nations in the Quran and their presence in the world, for the nations are remembered in the word of God while their bodies are gone from the earth, and the believer is taught that the remembrance in the Quran is the survival that matters, and that the nations survive as warnings, not as peoples. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to see the emptiness of the world's seats of power, for the thrones of 'Ad are empty and their peoples are gone, and the believer is taught to build what endures, the faith that survives the passing of the peoples.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to answer the question of the verse with the reflection it intends, to look upon the nations of the past and to see the emptiness, and to know that the one who builds upon the truth is the one whose trace remains, while the one who builds upon the might is the one whom the wind erases.",
  "9": "“Also, Pharaoh and those before him, and ˹the people of˺ the overturned cities ˹of Lot˺ indulged in sin,” moves from 'Ad to the rest of the destroyed nations, and the verse gathers Pharaoh and the others into the single account of the denial. “Also, Pharaoh and those before him, and the people of the overturned cities of Lot indulged in sin,” or “wa ja'a Fir'awnu wa man qablahu wal-mu'tafikatu bil-khati'ah.” Ibn Kathir explains that Pharaoh came with his sin, and those before him, and the overturned cities of Lot's people, and the believer is taught that the nations of the denial are one nation in their sin, and that the verse gathers them so that the hearer may see the breadth of the judgment, and that the denial of the truth is the one sin that unites the tyrant of Egypt and the people of the overturned cities. Al-Tabari notes the word “al-mu'tafikah” — “the overturned” — for the cities of Lot's people, and the believer is taught that the name of the cities is their punishment, and that the cities which overturned the natural order were themselves overturned.\n\nAl-Razi observes the word “bil-khati'ah” — “with the sin” — for the verse says that Pharaoh and the others came with the sin, and the believer is taught that the sin is the burden that the sinner carries to his judgment, and that the nations of the denial came to their end carrying the sin that had become their nature, and that the one who comes with the sin is met with the punishment of the sin. The verse also teaches the believer the unity of the judgment, for the nations differed in their lands and their times and their sins, and the judgment was one, and the believer is taught to see the one law in the many accounts, and to know that the one response to the truth decides the one end. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to fear the sin that is carried, for the sin that is carried to the judgment is the sin that is weighed at the judgment, and the believer is taught to cast off the sin by the repentance before the carrying is complete.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read the gathering of the nations as the gathering of the warning, for Pharaoh and 'Ad and Thamud and Lot's people are one warning in many voices, and the believer is taught to hear the one warning and to make the one response, and to know that the one response is the faith that saves from the one judgment.",
  "10": "“each disobeying their Lord’s messenger, so He seized them with a crushing grip.” states the common sin of the nations and the common punishment, and the verse reduces the whole account to its two elements. “Each disobeying their Lord's messenger, so He seized them with a crushing grip,” or “fa-'asaw rasoola rabbihim fa-akhathahum akhthatan rabiyah.” Ibn Kathir explains that each nation disobeyed the messenger of its Lord, and the believer is taught that the disobedience of the messenger is the one sin that the verse names of them all, and that the disobedience of the messenger is the disobedience of the One who sent him, and that the nations were destroyed not for their many sins but for the one root of them all. Al-Tabari notes the word “rabiyah” — “crushing,” “increasing,” “exceeding” — and the believer is taught that the grip of God was a grip that surpassed every measure, and that the punishment was fitted to the defiance, the crushing grip answering the crushing of the command.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the two elements, for the verse teaches the believer that the account of the nations is the account of the disobedience and the grip, and that the one who disobeys the messenger is met by the grip, and that the one who obeys is spared it, and the believer is taught to make his relation to the messenger the deciding relation of his life, and to know that the messenger is the door to the obedience and the shield from the grip. The verse also teaches the believer the severity of the divine seizure, for the word “akhthah” — “seizure” — is the word of the sudden taking, and the believer is taught that the seizure of God is not the slow punishment of men but the sudden grip that ends the defiance, and that the one who persists in the defiance is the one whom the grip awaits. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to obey the messenger of his Lord, for the messenger is the voice of the Lord, and the believer is taught to know that the disobedience of the voice is the invocation of the grip.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to let the account of the nations teach him the one lesson, for the disobedience of the messenger and the grip of the Lord are the two ends of the one path, and the believer is taught to walk the path of obedience, and to know that the obedience is the safety from the grip that the verse records.",
  "11": "“Indeed, when the floodwater had overflowed, We carried you in the floating Ark ˹with Noah˺,” turns from the punishment of the nations to the mercy that carried the believers, and the verse recalls the ark of Noah as the vessel of the salvation. “Indeed, when the floodwater had overflowed, We carried you in the floating Ark with Noah,” or “inna lamma tagha al-ma'u hamalnakum fi al-jariyah.” Ibn Kathir explains that when the floodwater rose and overflowed, God carried the believers in the floating ark, and the believer is taught that the same water that drowned the deniers carried the believers, and that the mercy of God is woven into the judgment, and that the ark is the sign of the salvation that God provides in the midst of the destruction. Al-Tabari notes the word “tagha” — “overflowed,” “exceeded” — for the water exceeded its bounds as the people had exceeded theirs, and the believer is taught that the punishment came in the form of the excess, the water overflowing as the sin had overflowed, and the believer is taught to fear the excess of the sin as he fears the excess of the water.\n\nAl-Razi observes the address of the verse, for the verse says “We carried you,” addressing the hearers through the memory of their ancestors, and the believer is taught that the salvation of Noah was the salvation of humanity, and that the believer is the heir of the ark, and that the ark is the sign that God saves His servants even when the world is drowning. The verse also teaches the believer the lesson of the ark, for the ark was built under the mockery of the deniers and became the refuge of the believers, and the believer is taught that the work of obedience, mocked by the heedless, is the work that saves, and that the believer who builds in obedience is carried when the waters rise. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to take the ark of the faith, for the flood of the judgment is the flood that the ark alone survives, and the believer is taught to enter the ark by the obedience, and to know that the ark is the mercy that God has prepared for His servants.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the ark as the sign of the salvation, for the ark carried the believers through the flood that drowned the deniers, and the believer is taught to make his faith his ark, and to know that the one who is in the ark of the faith is the one whom the flood of the judgment cannot drown.",
  "12": "“so that We may make this a reminder to you, and that attentive ears may grasp it.” states the purpose of the preservation of the ark and the story, and the verse closes the account with the reason it was told. “So that We may make this a reminder to you, and that attentive ears may grasp it,” or “li-naj'alaha lakum tathkiratan wa ta'iyaha uthunun wa'iyah.” Ibn Kathir explains that the story of the ark and the nations was preserved so that it might be a reminder, and the believer is taught that the events of the past are kept in the Quran as reminders, and that the reminder profits the one who has an attentive ear, and that the “attentive ear” — “uthun wa'iyah” — is the ear that hears and keeps, the ear that grasps the lesson and holds it. Al-Tabari notes that the phrase “attentive ears” is the Quran's praise of the ear that retains, and the believer is taught that the hearing of the Quran is a hearing that must be kept, and that the one who hears and forgets has not heard.\n\nAl-Razi observes the word “wa'iyah” — “attentive,” “retaining” — and explains that the attentive ear is the ear that understands and preserves, and the believer is taught to be of those whose ears are attentive, who hear the reminder and let it sink into the heart and remain, and that the ear which hears and lets the lesson pass is the ear that has heard the sound and missed the meaning. The verse also teaches the believer the purpose of the Quran's histories, for the histories are told to be reminders, and the believer is taught to read them as addressed to himself, and to know that the reminder is the mercy that the Quran extends, and that the one who takes the reminder is the one who is saved from the fates that the histories record. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to prepare his ear for the Quran, to listen with the attention of one who will keep what he hears, and to know that the attentive ear is the gift of God to the one who seeks it.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to make his ear attentive to the reminder, for the reminder of the ark and the nations is the reminder of the mercy and the judgment, and the believer is taught to grasp it and to hold it, and to know that the one whose ear is attentive is the one whose heart is safe.",
  "13": "\"At last, when the Trumpet will be blown with one blast,\" shifts from Ark reminder to the future Inevitable, the moment the reminder prepares for. The verse begins the final act after historical punishments.\n\n“At last, when the Trumpet will be blown with one blast” translates “fa-idha nufikha fi al-sur nafkhatun wahidah,” when the Horn is blown with a single blast. Trumpet, or “sur,” is the Horn entrusted to the angel Israfil, whose single blast signals the Inevitable Hour, the Striking Disaster, the Inevitable Event when earth is shaken violently. One blast emphasizes ease for Allah, whose command descends between seven heavens and whose creation of seven heavens is without flaw, who can reduce a garden to ashes while they slept with a single visitor. One blast suffices to end the smoothed earth’s walk and the adorned lamps’ watch, just as one word “Be” creates.\n\nThis follows attentive ears grasping the Ark reminder, indicating that one who grasped will hear this blast differently than those who thought every cry was against them like planks. For the attentive, the blast is the appointment that never delays and whose knowledge is with Allah alone; for the diverted, it is the torment drawing near with gloomy faces.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to picture the Trumpet as mere horn of folklore. The Quran presents it as real, heralding the crushing that follows, the torn sky, the Throne borne by eight, and the presentation where none of your secrets stay hidden.\n\nFor practice, live as one who expects one blast. The test of best deeds is ongoing, the pen writes, wealth and children test, and the Inevitable is one blast away, whether soon as garden owners’ morning or far as fifty thousand years. Hearing that one blast now through revelation should do what the warner’s coming does, humble the heart before it hardens, make you among those who protect families and walk upright, so the single blast finds you with light ahead, not with eyes downcast covered with disgrace.",
  "14": "\"and the earth and mountains will be lifted up and crushed with one blow,\" continues the Trumpet scene with the immediate physical effect. The verse uses one blow, like one blast, to show ease and totality.\n\n“and the earth and mountains will be lifted up and crushed with one blow” translates “wa humilat al-ardu wa al-jibalu fa-dukkata dakkatan wahidah,” earth and mountains are carried, lifted, then pounded into one crushing. Lifted indicates they are raised from their foundation, the smoothed earth you were told to move about and the mountains like tufts of wool on that Day, now not swallowed from below but lifted from above. Crushed with one blow parallels the garden reduced to ashes with one visitor while they slept and the Blast that destroyed Thamud with one overwhelming. One blow suffices because the One who created seven heavens in layers and is Most Capable of everything does not need repeated effort.\n\nThis follows one blast of the Trumpet, showing that the Inevitable Event will have come to pass, the sky torn and frail, angels on its sides, eight bearing the Throne. The smoothed earth that was mercy becomes the site of crushing, yet for those who were mindful to best ability, that crushing is passage to Gardens where they will be told to eat and drink joyfully for what they did in days gone by.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read lifting and crushing as mere natural earthquake without divine will. The Quran attributes it to the Inevitable Hour, the Striking Disaster denied by Thamud and ‘Ad, the crushing grip that seized Pharaoh and the overturned cities, all unleashed by Allah, the Most Compassionate who holds birds yet can withhold.\n\nFor reflection, consider that the earth you walk and the mountains you see as flawless creation will be lifted and crushed with one blow, so do not feel secure that the One in heaven will not cause earth to swallow or stones to unleash. Instead, move about regions and eat provision with thanks, be in awe without seeing, and prepare for the presentation where none of your secrets stay hidden, so the one blow lifts you toward honour in Gardens held in honour, not toward the raging Flame.",
  "15": "\"on that Day the Inevitable Event will have come to pass.\" names the result of one blast and one crushing, linking back to the surah’s opening. The verse is short, declarative, and final.\n\n“on that Day the Inevitable Event will have come to pass” translates “fa-yawma’idhin waqa‘at al-waqi‘ah,” the Inevitable will have occurred. Waqi‘ah, as at the start of Surah 56, “When the Inevitable Event takes place,” debases some and elevates others, shakes earth violently, scatters mountains to dust, sorts into three groups. Here it will have come to pass, confirming that the promise that “is easy for Allah” and the warning that “if only they knew the punishment of Hereafter is far worse” are now reality, no longer future threat that makes faces gloomy, but present fact.\n\nThat Day is the Day the sky will be torn frail, angels on sides, Throne borne by eight, you presented with no secrets hidden, records given to right hands who cry “Read my record!” and to left hands who cry “Would that I had not been given my record, would that death had been the end.” It is the Day of Gathering for mutual loss and gain, the Day the disbelievers asked “When will this threat come?” and were told knowledge is with Allah alone.\n\nThis follows one blow to show that the Inevitable is not a series of maybe, but a single coming-to-pass.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat Inevitable Event as one among many possible events that might be avoided by powerless forces. No force will help instead of the Most Compassionate, and oaths binding until the Day are false, so occurring is certain.\n\nFor the living, the inevitability is mercy through warning. Since it will have come to pass, act before it does. Be among the righteous who will have Gardens of Bliss, be submitted not wicked, judge rightly, walk upright, give rightful share, and be attentive ears that grasp the Ark reminder, so that when that Day comes, it is your ultimate triumph, not your crushing.",
  "16": "\"The sky will then be so torn that it will be frail,\" describes the heavens after the earth and mountains are crushed with one blow. The flawless creation that you were told to look at again and again without finding flaw now shows frailty at divine command.\n\n“The sky will then be so torn that it will be frail” translates “wa-nshaqqati al-sama’ fa-hiya yawma’idhin wahiyah,” the sky splits, becomes weak, feeble. Torn indicates rifting, as with adorned lowest heaven with lamps now split, and as with the sky will be like molten brass on the Day fifty thousand years. Frail indicates it loses the firmness that held it without flaw, similar to mountains like tufts of wool and earth swallowed as it quakes.\n\nThis follows the Inevitable Event having come to pass, showing the heavens share the earth’s fate, not exempt. The seven heavens created one above another in layers, without flaw, now torn, demonstrates that flawless does not mean indestructible; it means perfectly made for its term, then perfectly undone for the next. The same Most Capable who holds all authority and created death and life to test also tears and will recreate.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think torn sky contradicts “you will never see any flaw.” Flawlessness was for its appointed term, as provision never imagined and ease after hardship have terms; tearing is not flaw but fulfillment of the command descending between heavens that you were told to know proves He is Most Capable and encompasses all knowledge.\n\nThis precedes angels on its sides and eight bearing the Throne, showing that even as heavens tear, a higher order remains, the Throne.\n\nFor reflection, if the sky that you see as flawless will be frail, then the planks that impress, the merchandise fanfare, and the honour that expels inferior are even more frail. Do not feel secure that the One in heaven will not unleash stones or swallow earth; instead, be in awe without seeing, hear and obey to best ability, spend, and protect families, so when the sky is torn, you are not among those with eyes downcast covered with disgrace, but among those whose light shines ahead and who are told to eat and drink joyfully.",
  "17": "\"with the angels on its sides. On that Day eight ˹mighty angels˺ will bear the Throne of your Lord above them.\" places angelic order within the torn sky, following the Inevitable Event. The verse gives a glimpse of majesty at the height of upheaval.\n\n“with the angels on its sides” translates “wa al-malaku ‘ala arjaiha,” angels at its edges, its sides, after the sky is torn frail. Angels previously were missiles’ targets when eavesdropping devils were stoned, now they are stationed at the torn edges, orderly, not scattered, fulfilling “who never disobey whatever Allah orders—always doing as commanded,” like the formidable severe angels over the Fire. Their positioning shows that even as heavens weaken, angelic obedience holds, similar to mountains crushed yet the command descending remains.\n\n“On that Day eight mighty angels will bear the Throne of your Lord above them” translates “wa yahmilu ‘arsha rabbika fawqahum yawma’idhin thamaniyah,” eight will bear the Throne of your Lord above them. Throne, or “‘arsh,” is the greatest creation, symbol of authority in Whose Hands rests all authority, established upon after creating heavens and earth in six Days. Eight mighty angels bearing it signifies immense grandeur, as with the kingdom that is His and all praise for Him. The One who is with you wherever you are and nearer than you but you cannot see when the soul reaches the throat is now manifest in Throne borne above.\n\nThis follows sky torn to show that beyond torn heavens lies Throne that is not torn, and precedes you will be presented and none of your secrets will stay hidden, indicating that the Throne’s presence frames judgment, as with the wall with a gate between grace and torment.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to imagine angels physically supporting Allah as needing support, or to liken Throne to earthly seat. The Quran says establishment on the Throne without likening, and bearing is honour given to angels, not need of the Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy whose Hands hold authority.\n\nFor awe, remember the eight bearers when you feel impressed by plank-like appearance or caravan fanfare. No impressive human gathering compares to eight angels bearing the Throne on the Day the Inevitable has come to pass, and your presentation before Him will be with no secret hidden, so prepare by being among those given records in right hands who cry happily.",
  "18": "\"You will then be presented ˹before Him for judgment˺, and none of your secrets will stay hidden.\" reveals the personal climax after cosmic upheaval, torn sky, angels on sides, and Throne borne by eight. The verse moves from heavens to you.\n\n“You will then be presented before Him for judgment” translates “yawma’idhin tu‘radun,” you will be exposed, displayed, presented. Presentation is universal, all earlier and later generations, Thamud and ‘Ad, Pharaoh and the overturned, the garden owners and the Meccans tested, the hypocrites who said we bear witness, the wealth-diverted, the mindful and the rebelling, all dispersed over earth then gathered. Presented echoes the wall between grace and torment where groups are separated after light distinguishes, and the Day mutual loss and gain.\n\n“and none of your secrets will stay hidden” translates “la takhfa minkum khafiyah,” no hidden thing among you will be hidden. Secrets include whispering to exclude poor, secret boycott to make emigrants break away, false oaths as shields, impressive speech masking plank worthlessness, private withholding of good, and also secret charity, hidden awe without seeing, quiet making room, and sincere repentance that perfects light. All that was known to the Knower of seen and unseen, the Most Subtle, All-Aware who knows whether you speak secretly or openly, is now made manifest.\n\nThis follows crushing, Inevitable, sky torn, angels, Throne, showing sequence. Presentation is the purpose of gathering, as with “We will inform you of what you have done and that is easy for Allah.”\n\nA misunderstanding would be to fear presentation as mere exposure without mercy. Presentation for those in awe without seeing includes forgiveness and mighty reward, light ahead and to the right, and eating and drinking joyfully for days gone by.\n\nFor preparation, live as one who will be presented with no secret hidden. Align secret and open, speak goodness in private for righteousness, protect families, give rightful share, guard chastity, be true to trusts, and remain attentive ears to the Ark reminder, so the presentation you anticipate is the happy cry “Read my record, I knew I would face my reckoning” in a life of bliss, not the bitter cry “Would that I had not been given my record.”",
  "19": "\"As for those given their records in their right hand, they will cry ˹happily˺, \"Here ˹everyone˺! Read my record!\"\" introduces the joyful reception after presentation where no secret stays hidden. The scene contrasts the earlier gloom of disbelievers when torment draws near.\n\n“As for those given their records in their right hand” translates the people of the right, those whose light shines ahead and to the right, whose Gardens are bliss, whose faces are not gloomy. Right hand indicates honour, ease, and the side of the righteous who walk upright, as opposed to the left side whose residents become Fire. Records given in right hands are the well-preserved accounts that Allah kept while they forgot, now returned.\n\n“they will cry happily, ‘Here everyone! Read my record!’” translates “ha’um uqra’u kitabiyah,” come, read my book. The happiness is overt, generous, inviting all to see, unlike the whisper to exclude poor or secret talks that grieve. The joy is public because the record contains faith, hearing and obeying to best ability, spending, protecting families, being in awe without seeing, and the good loan multiplied. The invitation echoes the helpers who loved emigrants and preferred them, now inviting everyone to share joy, and contrasts with worthless planks who thought every cry against them.\n\nThis follows presentation and precedes “I surely knew I would face my reckoning,” showing why they are happy. They knew reckoning was coming, so they sent deeds forth for tomorrow, donated before death’s delay cry, and made room.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think right-hand recipients are sinless. They will have been forgiven, their sins absolved, their light perfected, as sincere repentance promises.\n\nFor today, aim for the right-hand cry. Be among the submitted not wicked, whose judgment you already know will distinguish, whose scripture you read is the Reminder for the whole world, and whose deeds the pen writes truthfully. Live so your record, when presented and no secret hidden, is one you will happily invite everyone to read, trusting the Most Appreciative who multiplies and forgives to make that joyful invitation yours on the Day the Inevitable has come to pass.",
  "20": "\"I surely knew I would face my reckoning.\"\" gives the reason for the right-hand recipient’s happiness after crying “Read my record!” The verse voices inner certainty that shaped outer deeds.\n\n“I surely knew I would face my reckoning” translates “inni zanantu anni mulaqin hisabiyah,” I thought, I was certain I would meet my account. Zanantu here is conviction, not doubt, as with those in awe without seeing who lived as if seeing. Knowing reckoning would come led them to listen and reason, to move about earth and eat provision knowing to Him is resurrection, to not feel secure that earth will not swallow or stones unleash, and to not be lost in delusion that a powerless force will help.\n\nThis certainty contrasts with garden owners who left no thought for Allah’s Will, hypocrites who thought every cry was against them but did not prepare for the Keeper’s cry, and the deniers who asked “When will this threat come if you are truthful?” as if it never would. The right-hand knowers lived with the Trumpet’s one blast in mind, the sky torn, the Throne borne, and presentation with no secrets hidden.\n\nThe phrase follows “Here, read my record!” to show that joy is not surprise, but fulfillment of expectation, as with those who believed and did good expecting Gardens under rivers for ever and ever, that is ultimate triumph.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear this as fatalistic dread. It is hopeful certainty, like the garden owners’ final hope “Our Lord will give us better for we are turning with hope,” but earlier and steady, not after ashes.\n\nFor practice, cultivate the same certainty. Say each morning that you will be presented and no secret hidden, then let that guide hearing, sight, intellect thanks, Friday diligence, and family protection. The One who knows best what is hidden and who holds birds will see that certainty produce best deeds, so the reckoning you face is the one you prepared for, leading to a life of bliss you will be told to eat and drink joyfully for days gone by.",
  "21": "\"They will be in a life of bliss.\" summarizes the state of those who knew they would face reckoning and whose records are in right hands. The verse is short, definitive, and follows the joyful invitation.\n\n“They will be in a life of bliss” translates “fa-huwa fi ‘ishatin radiyah,” he is in a pleasing, satisfying life, content, approved. Bliss here is “radiyah,” life that is pleased and pleasing, accepted by its owner and by its Lord, contrasting with the raging Flame that rips off scalps summoning those who turned away and hoarded, and with deprivation after harvest became chaff where planters lamented “we have been deprived.” Bliss is the Garden’s elevated life, as next specified, where provision is excellent and never withheld, where no poor is excluded today, and where honour belongs to Allah and His Messenger.\n\nThis follows knowing reckoning, showing that knowledge leads to bliss, not gloom. The blissful life is the same as Gardens of Bliss promised to the righteous, as the ultimate triumph on the Day of mutual loss and gain, and as the never-ending reward for the man of outstanding character.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to reduce bliss to material ease alone. The Quran pairs life of bliss with being presented with no secrets hidden and being told to eat and drink joyfully for days gone by, indicating bliss includes integrity, light perfected, and nearness.\n\nFor reflection, choose the life of bliss now in preparation. The smoothed earth you move about and eat from is a foretaste; use it with thanks, make room, lend good loans, and walk upright, so the life you are in after the Inevitable has come to pass is the blissful one where faces are not gloomy but are in Gardens held in honour, where the Pen wrote your record as pleasing and the Most Compassionate who holds all authority declares it pleasing too.",
  "22": "\"in an elevated Garden.\" specifies the location of the life of bliss for those given records in right hands. The phrase elevates literally and morally.\n\n“in an elevated Garden” translates “fi jannatin ‘aliyah,” a Garden high, lofty, sublime. Elevated indicates height above the raging Flame that scalps, above the torn frail sky, above the swallowed earth and unleashed stones. Height also indicates rank, as with thrones on which the righteous recline face to face, and elevated furnishings for the people of the right, and the Gardens of Bliss with their Lord for the mindful.\n\nElevated Garden contrasts with the low, fixated proceeding of garden owners who went early with hard purpose to exclude poor, and with the worthless planks leaned against a wall that are low despite impressive appearance. The righteous Garden is elevated because its owners were humble, in awe without seeing, and walked upright on the Straight Path, not crawling facedown.\n\nThis follows life of bliss to locate it, and precedes “whose fruit will hang within reach,” showing that elevation does not mean inaccessibility; fruit is near.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think elevated means distant or difficult. The Quran says fruit hangs within reach, provision is excellent, and movement is at ease, as earth was smoothed for you.\n\nFor practice, elevation is achieved through lowliness now, being mindful to best ability, hearing and obeying, spending, protecting families, and turning in sincere repentance so light shines. The One who bears the Throne above eight angels can elevate your Garden above the Blaze, so strive with wealth and lives as best for you, trusting the Almighty, All-Forgiving who created death and life to test which is best in deeds to place you in the elevated Garden where you will be held in honour, not in the Flame that summons those who hoarded.",
  "23": "\"whose fruit will hang within reach.\" describes the accessibility of the elevated Garden’s provision. The verse assures that height does not mean hardship.\n\n“whose fruit will hang within reach” translates “qutufuha daniyah,” its clusters near, low, easy to pick. Daniyah indicates clusters hanging close to the hand, as with abundant fruit never out of season nor forbidden and clusters of bananas for the people of the right, and as with thornless lote trees and flowing water. Near fruit contrasts with the garden owners’ harvest that became ashes and required early going to pick all, and with merchandise that required leaving the pulpit to chase.\n\nThis follows elevated Garden to correct the assumption that elevated is distant. The elevated are still within reach because the Most Compassionate who holds birds and smooths earth also makes provision near, and because the righteous walked upright, not crawled, so they can reach without stooping.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat near fruit as mere indulgence. Near fruit is honour, as with eating and drinking joyfully for days gone by, a recompense for best deeds, for giving rightful share to beggar and poor, and for guarding chastity within lawful bonds.\n\nFor reflection, the near fruit invites taste now through rightful share. Give fruit that hangs within reach to the poor today, let the poor enter your garden today, and let the rightful share hang within reach of beggar and poor in your wealth, so the Garden you are elevated to has fruit that remains within reach for ever and ever, where the Pen wrote your generosity and the Most Appreciative multiplied it.\n\nThe nearness of fruit also teaches about provision’s source. Gardens of pulsating shade and flowing water are not self-sustaining; they are granted by the Lord of all worlds who smoothed the earth for you and who can make water sink. Near fruit that is always within reach reflects the Most Appreciative who multiplies the good loan and the Most Merciful whose Hands hold all authority, the same Hands that hold birds aloft and that descended command between seven heavens. When you see fruit hanging low in the world, whether a market display or a neighbour’s tree, remember the elevated Garden where fruit hangs even lower, and let that remembrance move you to let the poor enter your garden today, to consult courteously on nursing, and to provide according to means, so near fruit now becomes practice for near fruit forever.",
  "24": "\"˹They will be told,˺ \"Eat and drink joyfully for what you did in the days gone by.\"\" records the hospitality spoken to those in the elevated Garden whose fruit hangs within reach. The words turn bliss into a feast of remembrance.\n\n“They will be told, ‘Eat and drink joyfully for what you did in the days gone by’” translates “kulu wa ishrabū hani’an bi-ma aslaftum fi al-ayyam al-khaliyah,” eat and drink with wholesome enjoyment because of what you sent forth in former days. Hani’an is pleasant, without harm, like the drink in Gardens that causes no headache, the provision that never withholds, and the excellent provision granted to those who believe and do good.\n\n“For what you did in the days gone by” links Garden to worldly days, the smoothed earth days when you moved and ate provision, dispersed then gathered, heard the warner and listened, looked again at flawless heavens, gave rightful share, protected families, walked upright, and turned in sincere repentance. Days gone by are the test days of wealth and children, the Friday you left business to proceed diligently, the garden you said “Allah willing” over, the trusts you kept and prayers you observed.\n\nThis follows elevated Garden with near fruit, adding the spoken welcome that perfects light. It mirrors the wall where grace on one side is told “go back and seek light” to others, but here grace is told to eat and drink.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think eating and drinking are earned wages that obligate the King. They are excellent provision granted, favour He grants to whoever He wills, Lord of infinite bounty, yet linked to days gone by to show justice.\n\nFor practice, make days gone by worthy of this telling. Eat and drink now with thanks, using hearing, sight, intellect hardly thanked, and send forth good for the elevated Garden, so the One who will present you with no secret hidden tells you to eat and drink joyfully, and your life of bliss is confirmed as He is with you wherever you are and who knows what is hidden, now rewarding openly what was done sincerely in days gone by.",
  "25": "\"And as for those given their record in their left hand, they will cry ˹bitterly˺, \"I wish I had not been given my record.\"\" begins the counterpart to the right-hand joy. The verse moves from eat and drink joyfully to bitter wish.\n\n“And as for those given their record in their left hand” indicates the opposite side, the people of the left whose accommodation is boiling water and burning, whose faces are gloomy when torment draws near, whose disgrace covers downcast eyes when asked to prostrate but they cannot. Left hand is disgrace, as right is honour.\n\n“they will cry bitterly, ‘I wish I had not been given my record’” translates the lament that contrasts “Read my record!” The left-hand wish is to have remained hidden, as with “Do you see any of them left alive?” after ‘Ad, but now they themselves wish their record had stayed hidden, though on this Day no secret stays hidden and He will inform you of what you did. The wish echoes that of the garden owners before hope, but here without the sensible urging to say Allah willing.\n\nThis follows the Garden’s hospitality to show two hospitality outcomes. One is eating joyfully for days gone by; the other is wishing the record had never come, because days gone by were spent withholding good, transgressing, saying ancient fables, and persisting in arrogance and aversion.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear this as mere regret without moral content. The wish is tied to specific deeds, as upcoming verses list “My wealth has not benefited me, my authority stripped, seizing, burning, chains seventy arms, they never had faith in the Greatest nor encouraged feeding of poor.”\n\nFor the living, the way to avoid crying “I wish I had not been given my record” is to cry now “I knew I would face my reckoning” and live accordingly, being among those in awe without seeing, whose mighty reward is forgiveness, who protect families, give rightful share, and walk upright, so the record given is the one you will happily invite everyone to read.",
  "26": "\"nor known anything of my reckoning!\" continues the left-hand bitter cry after “I wish I had not been given my record.” The wish deepens to wishing ignorance of reckoning.\n\n“nor known anything of my reckoning!” translates “wa lam adri ma hisabiyah,” I did not know what my account is. The left-hand speaker wishes the previous life’s dispersal over earth without gathering had been true, similar to “I wish death was the end!” next. Reckoning, or “hisab,” is the informing on the Day He gathers for mutual loss and gain, where He informs you of what you did and that is easy for Allah. Not knowing reckoning would mean never being presented where no secret stays hidden, never hearing the keepers ask about warners.\n\nThis follows the right-hand certainty “I surely knew I would face my reckoning” to contrast. One knew and prepared by sending deeds forth for tomorrow, donating before death, and walking upright; the other wished not to know and lived as if smoothed earth was for diversion rather than resurrection to Him, feeling secure that One in heaven will not swallow or unleash stones.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear this as mere fear of numbers. Not knowing reckoning is wishing to remain lost in delusion that a powerless force will help instead of the Most Compassionate and that provision will not be withheld.\n\nFor the living, the cure is to know reckoning now. Say with the right-hand, “I knew I would face it,” let that knowledge humble the heart before it hardens, revive it like earth, and make you among those in awe without seeing whose mighty reward is forgiveness, so when reckoning is known, it is not the unknown dread wished away but the known meeting prepared for with light ahead.\n\nThe wish not to know reckoning also exposes how diversion by wealth and children works. Wealth that was not lent as a good loan and children not raised to protect families become the very reasons reckoning is feared, as with the withholder who said “Do not let any poor enter today” and then could not eat himself. Not knowing would not remove the need to answer for the Balance sent so people may administer justice, for the Pen’s writing, and for the Light that would humble a mountain. The only way to not fear knowing is to know now, to welcome the warner, and to let “whether you speak secretly or openly He knows best what is hidden in the heart” become comfort rather than threat, because secret and open are aligned toward goodness.",
  "27": "\"I wish death was the end!\" voices the ultimate wish of the left-hand recipient after wishing not to have been given the record and not to have known reckoning. The cry reveals the worldview that denied resurrection.\n\n“I wish death was the end!” translates “ya laytaha kanat al-qadiyah,” would that it had been decisive, cut off. Qadiyah is the end that settles, like death being appointed time that never delays, which they thought was final. Wishing death was the end mirrors their claim “We will not be resurrected. Yes, by your Lord you will surely be resurrected, then you will be informed of what you have done and that is easy for Allah,” and the garden owners’ “We have been deprived” and the deniers’ “When we are dead and reduced to dust, will we really be resurrected?” The wish shows they structured life as if dispersal over earth had no gathering, as if wealth and children were not a test but ultimate honour.\n\nThis follows the presentation where no secret stays hidden, proving death was not the end, but a transition, as with the Ark carried and the Trumpet blown with one blast, earth and mountains crushed, sky torn, Throne borne, and all presented.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think death as end is neutral. The Quran calls this wish a lament of losers who let wealth and children divert from remembrance and who are true losers, not winners via merchandise.\n\nFor the living, avoid this wish by living as if death is not the end. Let the reminder that would humble a mountain remind you, move about regions and eat provision knowing to Him is your resurrection, give rightful share to beggar and poor, and be true to trusts, so death becomes not the end wished for, but the beginning of the elevated Garden where you are told to eat and drink joyfully for days gone by, and where the elevated life of bliss never wishes for cutting off.",
  "28": "\"My wealth has not benefited me!\" laments the left-hand holder after wishing death was the end. The cry names the first failed support.\n\n“My wealth has not benefited me!” translates “ma aghna ‘anni maliyah,” my wealth has not availed me, not enriched, not helped. Wealth here is the same wealth that diverted from remembrance, that was withheld as “Do not spend on those with the Messenger,” that boasted honour will expel inferior, that withheld good as despicable oath-taker, slanderer, gossip-monger. Wealth was the powerless force that would help instead of the Most Compassionate and that would provide if He withholds, but at the Day no close friend will ask about friends though they see each other, and wealth cannot ransom even by children, spouses, siblings, clan, and everyone on earth.\n\nThis follows wishing death was the end, showing why death being not the end devastates the wealthy withholder. While alive, wealth impressed like planks leaned worthless that impress and like merchandise fanfare that draws crowds from the pulpit; now it is worthless.\n\nThe cry mirrors earlier “neither wealth nor children will help against Allah whatsoever, they will be residents of the Fire for ever” for oath-shield hypocrites, and “no calamity befalls except by Allah’s Will” where trust in wealth is replaced by faith that guides hearts.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to condemn wealth itself. Wealth lent as good loan is multiplied and forgiven by the Most Appreciative, Most Forbearing, and the man of wealth providing according to means after hardship brings ease, shows wealth rightly used benefits, as with helpers who preferred emigrants despite need.\n\nFor practice, make wealth benefit you before it cannot. Do not let it divert from remembrance; spend in charity to best ability, give rightful share, lend good loans, and protect families, so the cry at presentation is not “My wealth has not benefited me” but “Read my record,” and wealth becomes part of the days gone by for which you are told to eat and drink joyfully, and your wealth’s test is passed as the great reward with Allah is secured.",
  "29": "\"My authority has been stripped from me.\"\" adds the second failed support after wealth. The left-hand speaker moves from wealth to power.\n\n“My authority has been stripped from me” translates “halaka ‘anni sultaniyah,” my authority, my proof, my power has perished, gone away. Sultan is authority, argument, dominion, the same honour and power claimed when they said “the honourable will definitely expel the inferior” and when they thought strongholds put them out of reach and that oaths until the Day would guarantee whatever they decide. Authority here includes political power like Pharaoh seized with crushing grip, social influence like the brute with wealth and children who said ancient fables, and legal argument like “Do you have a scripture in which you read that you will have whatever you choose?” All are stripped, as the earth and mountains are lifted and crushed with one blow and the sky is torn frail, leaving no place for authority to stand.\n\nThis follows wealth’s failure to show that both pillars of worldly security, money and power, collapse together, as with garden owners whose fixated purpose and whispered exclusion could not prevent ashes, and with ‘Ad’s seven nights and eight days of wind that left none alive.\n\nThe phrase completes the left-hand’s worldly inventory before the command “Seize and shackle them” follows. It mirrors the right-hand’s inventory that remains, such as faith, hearing and obeying, and light.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read authority stripped as merely losing office. It is loss of proof, the ability to argue “my oaths bind” or “my associate-gods will bring.” On the Day no secret stays hidden, no argument remains unless it was truth.\n\nFor the living, secure authority differently. Seek the authority that remains, being among the submitted not wicked, whose Gardens of Bliss with their Lord remain, whose life of bliss is pleasing, and whose rightful share and trusts were kept. Authority that will not be stripped is that granted by the Lord in Whose Hands rests all authority and who is Most Capable of everything, who created death and life to test which is best in deeds.",
  "30": "\"˹It will be said,˺ \"Seize and shackle them.\"\" records the command after the left-hand lament that wealth and authority have perished. The imperative is brief and severe, contrasting with “Eat and drink joyfully” told to the right-hand.\n\n“It will be said, ‘Seize and shackle them’” translates “khudhuhu fa-ghulluhu,” seize him and fetter him, place chains, as with the formidable severe angels who never disobey and who oversee the Fire whose fuel is people and stones. Seizing mirrors the crushing grip that seized Pharaoh and the overturned, the seizing by the right hand warned if the Messenger had invented, and the seizing of societies called to severe account. Shackling is the opposite of being carried in the Ark, of being held by the Most Compassionate as birds are held, and of being admitted into Gardens where fruit hangs within reach.\n\nThis follows authority stripped to show that power now belongs entirely to the Commander. The One who is with you wherever you are and nearer than you when soul reaches throat now commands, and the powerless force that would help instead of the Most Compassionate is absent.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear this as cruelty for its own sake. It is the recompense for persistent denial, for calling revelations ancient fables, for withholding good, and for not having faith in the Greatest nor encouraging feeding of poor, as next explained.\n\nFor reflection, the way to avoid being seized and shackled is to be among those who give rightful share, guard chastity, keep trusts, and pray consistently, whose Gardens are held in honour and whose record will be read happily. The pen writes either way; let it write seizing avoided through sincere repentance that perfects light, not through wealth or authority that will be stripped.\n\nSeizing and shackling also mirror the earlier causes. The withholder of good who said “We will harvest all” is seized as he seized provision from the poor; the slanderer who cut with eyes is shackled as he cut with gossip; the brute who transgressed is bound as he bound others with false shields. The formidable severe angels over the Fire who never disobey execute what the Pen wrote and what the well-preserved Record kept, showing that the Balance sent for justice is not symbolic. To avoid this seizing, be among those who make room in gatherings, who lend good loans, who guard chastity, and who are attentive ears that grasp the Ark reminder, so the only seizing you know is being carried in the Ark and held as birds are held by the Most Compassionate.",
  "31": "\"then burn them in Hell.\" continues the command after seizing and shackling. The sentence is stark, matching the raging Flame described elsewhere.\n\n“then burn them in Hell” translates “thumma al-jahima salluhu,” then roast him in the Blaze, make him enter and be exposed to Hell’s heat. Hell here is “Jahim,” the intensely hot, blazing Fire that almost bursts in fury, that roars as it boils over, that rips off scalps, that is the prepared torment for eavesdropping devils and for those who disbelieve in their Lord, whose evil destination was warned. Burning follows shackling, as with keepers asking and presentation, showing ordered procedure, not chaos.\n\nThis follows seizing to show that shackling is not mere restraint but prelude to roasting, as earth and mountains crushed and sky torn precede presentation. The One who smoothed earth for you to move and eat and adorned lowest heaven with lamps now shows the Fire that devils’ stoning guards.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read burning as disproportionate for withholding. The next verses explain that burning follows not having faith in the Greatest and not encouraging feeding of poor, which is rejecting the two pillars of relationship with Creator and creation, persisting in arrogance and aversion, and being among the wicked not submitted.\n\nFor the living, the burning is warning that perfects awe without seeing, whose mighty reward is forgiveness. To avoid it, be in awe without seeing, believe in the Light, give rightful share, encourage feeding, protect families, and walk upright, so the command heard is “Eat and drink joyfully” in the elevated Garden, not “then burn them in Hell.” The choice is made in days gone by, where the smoothed earth you move upon is still the place to turn.\n\nBurning in Hell also contrasts with the Garden’s burning that was avoided. The garden owners’ garden burned while they slept as a worldly burning; the Fire’s burning is far worse, as “if only they knew.” The garden burned because they excluded the poor; Hell burns because they excluded faith in the Greatest and encouragement of feeding the poor, the two pillars that would have made them among those in awe without seeing whose mighty reward is forgiveness. The One who adorned the lowest heaven with lamps as missiles against devils now shows the Fire those missiles guard against. To avoid being burned, be among the mindful who fear Allah to best ability, hear and obey, spend, and are saved from selfishness, whose Gardens are held in honour and whose light shines ahead.",
  "32": "\"then tie them up with chains seventy arms long.\" details the shackling after seizing and burning. The length emphasizes total restraint.\n\n“then tie them up with chains seventy arms long” translates “thumma fi silsilatin dhar‘uha sab‘una dhira‘an fa-slukuhu,” then in a chain seventy cubits, make him enter or thread him. Seventy is a large, complete number indicating extensive, inescapable binding, as seven nights and eight days was extensive for ‘Ad, and as seventy arms shows chains that can encircle and drag, unlike the near fruit that hangs within reach for the righteous. Binding follows burning, showing Fire’s inhabitants are not free to move about regions as the smoothed earth once allowed.\n\nThis follows burning to show that Hell is not only heat but humiliation and helplessness, as with eyes downcast covered with disgrace when asked to prostrate but unable, and faces gloomy when torment draws near. The chain’s length mocks the authority stripped, where sultan perished; now the only sultan is the chain.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to debate chain mechanics literally, as with oaths binding. The Quran uses measurable image to convey inescapability, not to invite engineering speculation.\n\nFor reflection, the chain is the opposite of the trust and covenant that faithful keep, the true chains that bind them to righteousness willingly, and the light that shines ahead. To avoid this chain, bind yourself now to trusts, to hearing and obeying to best ability, to spending, and to sincere repentance, so the only chain you know is the firm rope of Allah that saves from selfishness and that the Most Appreciative appreciates.\n\nThe chain of seventy arms also contrasts with the elevated Garden’s ease. In the elevated Garden there is no chain, only fruit hanging within reach; in Hell, chains prevent any reaching. Seventy arms length indicates that the bound one is fully encircled, unable to move about regions as the smoothed earth once allowed, unable to proceed early with fixated purpose as garden owners did, and unable to proceed diligently to remembrance on Friday. The chain mocks the garden owners’ hard purpose and the Meccans’ hard bargain to extinguish light with mouths, showing hard without “Allah willing” becomes bound. To avoid this binding, bind yourself now to the firm rope of Allah, to trusts and covenants truly kept, to prayers properly observed, so the only length you know is the vastness of Paradise as vast as heavens and earth prepared for those who believe in Allah and His messengers.",
  "33": "\"For they never had faith in Allah, the Greatest.\" gives the first reason for seizing, shackling, burning, and chaining seventy arms. The verse explains cause.\n\n“For they never had faith in Allah, the Greatest” translates “innahu kana la yu’minu billahi al-‘azim,” he did not believe in Allah, the Greatest. Greatest, or “‘Azim,” is the One in Whose Hands rests all authority, Most Capable of everything, who created seven heavens without flaw, who holds birds, who is King, Holy, Almighty. Not having faith in the Greatest underlies calling revelations ancient fables, saying Allah revealed nothing, claiming oaths bind Him until the Day, and feeling secure that the One in heaven will not swallow or unleash stones.\n\nThis follows the chain to show that punishment is not for a single withheld meal, but for root disbelief, as with Thamud’s blast and ‘Ad’s wind for denying the Striking, and Pharaoh’s crushing grip for disobeying the messenger.\n\nThe Greatest also recalls “So glorify the Name of your Lord, the Greatest” that closes surahs about the Inevitable, inviting the opposite of this cause.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think mere verbal belief without acts suffices. The next verse adds “nor encouraged the feeding of the poor,” showing faith must include its social fruit.\n\nFor practice, have faith in Allah, the Greatest truly, which appears as putting trust in the Most Compassionate alone, believing in Light, walking upright, protecting families, and giving rightful share, so the Greatest who can crush earth and mountains with one blow can also admit you into the elevated Garden where you are told to eat and drink joyfully for days gone by, rather than chaining for lacking faith in His greatness.\n\nNot having faith in the Greatest also explains why the withholder could say ancient fables and pure magic with confidence. If the Greatest were truly recognized, the Pen’s oath, the mountain that would be humbled, and the seven heavens without flaw would have humbled him. The Greatest, or “Al-‘Azim,” is the One whose Hands hold all authority and whose command descends between seven heavens, who is with you wherever you are and nearer than you when the soul reaches the throat, yet you cannot see. To have faith in Him is to believe in Him alone we believe and in Him alone we trust, to hear and obey to best ability, and to spend so self is saved from selfishness. Without that faith, the greatest becomes the smallest in concern, and the seventy-arm chain becomes the only greatness known, a greatness of punishment that is far worse than the worldly ashes, if only they knew.",
  "34": "\"nor encouraged the feeding of the poor.\" adds the second reason for the chain after lacking faith in the Greatest. The crime is social neglect, not only creedal.\n\n“nor encouraged the feeding of the poor” translates “wa la yahuddu ‘ala ta‘ami al-miskin,” he did not urge feeding the needy. Encouraging, or “yahuddu,” is to prompt, to advocate, including encouraging others, as with “Do not spend on those with the Messenger so they break away” and “Do not let any poor enter your garden today,” which are the opposite, discouraging feeding. Feeding the poor is the rightful share for beggar and poor, lending good loan, letting the poor enter today, and providing according to means for waiting women. Not encouraging it reveals withholder of good, transgressor, evildoer, brute character.\n\nThis follows faith in the Greatest to show that faith without social mercy is deficient, as with hypocrites who said “we bear witness” but hindered from the Way, and as with garden owners who proceeded fixated without “Allah willing.”\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this condemns only the wealthy who can feed many. Encouraging feeding can be a word, a whisper for goodness, a plan to make room, a reminder to give.\n\nFor reflection, encourage feeding actively. Speak for the poor in your garden planning, ensure wealth does not merely circulate among rich, sponsor feeding through good loans, and protect families by teaching children to feed, so the chain of seventy arms is replaced by the near fruit that hangs within reach and the joyful eating for days gone by, and you are among the righteous who will have Gardens of Bliss with their Lord, not among those who withheld and were then tied.\n\nNot encouraging feeding of the poor also reveals a community failure, not only personal stinginess. Encouraging is public, whispers among one another as garden owners whispered “Do not let any poor enter today,” and hypocrites whispered “Do not spend on those with the Messenger.” The withholder does not merely neglect; he promotes withholding, making wealth circulate only among the rich, contrary to gains for Allah and the Messenger, orphans, poor, and needy travellers. This promotion is why he has no close friend on the Day; he isolated the poor, so he is isolated. Encouraging feeding now, even with a word that makes room and a reminder that provision is from sources never imagined, creates the close friends that will exist in the elevated Garden, where eating and drinking joyfully is communal, not solitary like oozing pus.",
  "35": "\"So this Day they will have no close friend here.\" states the social isolation on the Day following the two reasons. The verse moves from cause to consequence in fellowship.\n\n“So this Day they will have no close friend here” translates “fa-laysa lahu al-yawma hahuna hamim,” he will have no intimate, warm friend here today. This Day is the Day the Inevitable has come to pass, when Trumpet blown, earth crushed, sky torn, Throne borne, and presentation with no secrets hidden occurs. No close friend contrasts with “Eat and drink joyfully” fellowship of the right-hand, whose elevated Garden is communal, whose thrones are face to face, and whose greeting is peace. Here, the withholder of good who had no close friend in truth, only powerless forces and associate-gods that cannot be brought, now experiences true friendlessness.\n\nThis follows not encouraging feeding, showing that withholding from the poor isolates. The garden owners who excluded poor found themselves deprived with no helper; the hypocrites who promised to leave and help found no help; the brute with wealth and children who said ancient fables found his snout marked and no powerless force to help instead of the Most Compassionate.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as merely lonely feeling. It is social reality, as with “no close friend will ask about friends though they see each other” on the Day fifty thousand years, and with the Fire’s keepers, not friends, asking.\n\nFor the living, befriend now through feeding, making room, and standing for Allah as disciples stood, so the Day finds you with close friends in the Party of Allah bound to succeed, whose Gardens are held in honour, rather than friendless where even seventy-arm chains cannot be loosened by any intimate.\n\nNo close friend on that Day also mirrors the wall with a gate whose one side is grace and the other torment, where no hamim helps. The withholder thought his powerless force of wealth, children, clan that sheltered, and merchandise fanfare were close friends, but they are not hamim instead of the Most Compassionate. The Day the sky is molten brass and mountains are wool, no hamim asks, though they see each other, and the wicked wishes to ransom with children, spouses, siblings, clan, and everyone on earth. True hamim is the Party of Allah bound to succeed, the helpers who loved emigrants, the disciples who stood with Jesus, the attentive ears that grasp, and the righteous whose Gardens are with their Lord. To have a hamim on that Day, be a hamim now, give rightful share, protect families, and be in awe without seeing, so the close friend you need is already written by the Pen as your own good loan multiplied by the Most Appreciative.",
  "36": "\"nor any food except ˹oozing˺ pus.\" describes the nourishment on that Day after stating no close friend. The verse gives the only food.\n\n“nor any food except oozing pus” translates “wa la ta‘amu illa min ghislin,” no food except from washing, discharge, pus from the inhabitants’ wounds, as with hamim and ghassaq and the Zaqqum that chokes. Ghisl is filth washed from the punished, opposite to the near fruit that hangs within reach, the abundant fruit never out of season nor forbidden, and the joyful eating for days gone by. Food here is not nourishment but torment, as the withholder who excluded poor from garden now cannot eat, and the chain of seventy arms prevents reaching.\n\nThis follows no close friend to show total deprivation, social and nutritional, as with the Fire nearly bursting and the raging Flame ripping scalps. It also follows not encouraging feeding, showing recompense, you did not feed, so you will not be fed.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to imagine this as mere disgusting image. It is real recompense that makes the earlier threat “which of you is mad?” and “Should We treat the submitted like the wicked?” clear.\n\nFor reflection, the way to avoid oozing pus is to feed now. Give rightful share, let the poor enter your garden today, lend the good loan the Most Appreciative multiplies, and protect families from Fire whose fuel is people and stones, so your food on this Day is the elevated Garden’s near fruit and the joyful eating told for days gone by, with close friends around, not pus among the friendless.\n\nOozing pus as food also contrasts with the Garden’s excellent provision granted to those who believe and do good and admitted for ever and ever. Ghisl is the discharge of the punished, filthy and non-nourishing, opposite to the near fruit, the splendid homes, and the joyful eating for days gone by. Eating pus is the recompense for eating from withheld gardens and for feeding delusion that submitted and wicked are equal. The One who smooths earth for you to eat from His provisions and who can make water sink then bring flowing water now shows what eating is like when provision is withheld in Hell. To avoid this food, eat now from lawful provision with thanks, using hearing, sight, and intellect hardly thanked, and ensure others eat, letting the poor enter today, so your food on this Day is not ghisl that only evildoers eat, but the elevated Garden’s fruit that is excellent provision, granted by Him, not seized by Shaitan’s whispered exclusion.",
  "37": "\"which none will eat except the evildoers.\"\" completes the food description after stating no food except oozing pus. The verse limits eaters.\n\n“which none will eat except the evildoers” translates “la ya’kuluhu illa al-khati’un,” none eats it except the sinful, the offenders, the evildoers. Khati’un are those who missed the mark, who denied the Striking as Thamud and ‘Ad did, who indulged in sin as Pharaoh and the overturned cities, who disobeyed the messenger, who took the Reminder lightly as ancient fables, and who were among the wicked not submitted.\n\nThis follows oozing pus to show that this food is not for the mindful who protect families, not for those in awe without seeing whose mighty reward is forgiveness, not for those who give rightful share and guard chastity. Only the khati’un eat ghisl, as only the residents of Blaze eat hamim and only the chain bearers are tied.\n\nIt also parallels “which none will eat except the evildoers” with “No one will eat except...” exclusivity that mirrors Gardens exclusive to the righteous who will have Gardens of Bliss with their Lord, not equal to wicked.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think evildoer is permanent label without chance. The Quran shows garden owners were transgressors then turned with hope and were given chance for better garden; sincere repentance moves one from khati’un to righteous.\n\nFor practice, avoid being the eater of ghisl by not being the withholder. Feed the poor, encourage feeding, have faith in the Greatest, walk upright, and be among the attentive ears that grasp the Ark reminder, so the only food you know is the near fruit and the joyful eating for days gone by in the elevated Garden where you are in a life of bliss, not the pus that only evildoers eat on this Day.\n\nThis exclusivity also humbles the withholder who thought “Do not let any poor enter today” was clever. On that Day, the only eaters of pus are precisely those who withheld, while the Garden’s near fruit remains for the generous, showing that exclusion in this world becomes exclusion from mercy in the next, unless sincere repentance intervenes before the appointed time that never delays.",
  "38": "\"Now, I do swear by whatever you see.\" begins a new oath after describing the two foods, returning to the Quran’s defense. The surah now swears to affirm revelation after the Inevitable scenes.\n\n“Now, I do swear by whatever you see” translates “fa-la uqsimu bi-ma tubsirun,” I swear by what you see. Whatever you see includes the seven heavens you were told to look again at without finding flaw, the lowest heaven adorned with lamps, birds spreading and folding held by the Most Compassionate, the smoothed earth you move upon, the garden reduced to ashes, the Palm trunks like uprooted, the Ark carried, the Trumpet, the Throne borne. All that is visible testifies to the Inevitable.\n\nThis oath follows the food of evildoers to contrast seen and unseen, as the next verse adds whatever you cannot see, covering the well-preserved Record, the appointed time that never delays, and the Day fifty thousand years. Swearing by seen prepares to affirm that the Quran is the recitation of a noble Messenger, not poet or fortune-teller.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think oath by seen is mere poetry. Oath by seen is evidence, as with positions of stars as a great oath for the noble Quran.\n\nFor reflection, look at what you see, from flawless heavens to near fruit in elevated Gardens, and let seeing become swearing, affirming that the Inevitable is true, that the Garden and Fire foods are true, and that the Reminder for the whole world is true, so you become among those who listened and reasoned and will be told to eat joyfully, not among those who need pus.\n\nSeeing includes the flawless seven heavens you were told to look again at without finding flaw, the birds held aloft, the Garden reduced to ashes while they slept, the Palm trunks like uprooted, and the Ark carried. Seeing also includes the Inevitable’s signs, the Trumpet’s one blast and the mountains crushed. Swearing by what you see makes the oath checkable; you can look at what you see and see no flaw, then trust what the oath affirms. The unseen oath next completes it, but seeing alone already shows the Quran, which would humble a mountain, is not poet’s prose. For the living, seeing with the Pen’s help transforms ancient fables dismissal into “Glory be to our Lord, we have been wrongdoers” and then into “We trust our Lord will give us better,” so seeing becomes reasoning that saves from being among residents of Blaze who lament if only we had listened and reasoned.",
  "39": "\"and whatever you cannot see!\" completes the oath after “whatever you see.” The pair covers all existence, visible and hidden.\n\n“and whatever you cannot see!” translates “wa ma la tubsirun,” and what you do not see, the unseen that the Most Compassionate knows, the hidden in hearts, the unseen Record, the Day the Shin is bared, the Throne, the eight bearers, the Inevitable Hour, the Striking Disaster, and the promise that honour belongs to Allah. Whatever you cannot see includes the torment that struck while they slept reducing it to ashes, the horror cast on fortified hearts, and the provision from sources never imagined.\n\nThe oath by unseen follows seen to show completeness, as with Knower of seen and unseen, Almighty, All-Wise, and with the well-preserved Record touched only by purified angels.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think unseen is unknowable and therefore irrelevant. The Quran says unseen is known to the Knower and revealed through the noble Messenger’s recitation, so it is knowable via revelation.\n\nFor practice, swear by both what you see and what you do not see in your own life. You see smoothed earth, you do not see the earth swallowing if secure; you see merchandise fanfare, you do not see flowing water sinking; you see impressive planks, you do not see plank worthlessness. Let the oath guide you to trust the unseen promise that the righteous will have Gardens of Bliss and the wicked will have the Blaze, and to live accordingly, hearing and obeying to best ability, spending, and protecting families, so unseen becomes seen as light perfected on the Day you are presented and no secret stays hidden.\n\nThe unseen oath covers the well-preserved Record only purified angels touch, the appointed time that never delays, the Day fifty thousand years, the Shin bared, the Throne borne by eight, and the provision from sources never imagined. Unseen also includes the horror cast on fortified hearts from where they never expected and the torment that struck the garden while they slept. Swearing by unseen shows the revelation informs of what you cannot see but need, as with the Garden’s elevated bliss and the Fire’s chain of seventy arms. The Most Subtle, All-Aware knows the unseen you cannot see and the seen you can, and informs you via the noble Messenger’s recitation. For practice, trust the unseen promise as you trust the seen sign, lend the good loan you see diminish but unseen multiplies, protect families you see now for the Garden you do not yet see, and walk upright on the Straight Path unseen but sure, so unseen becomes your perfected light on the Day you are presented and no secret stays hidden.",
  "40": "\"Indeed, this ˹Quran˺ is the recitation of a noble Messenger.\" states what the oath by seen and unseen supports. The verse defends revelation after depicting the Inevitable and its foods.\n\n“Indeed, this Quran is the recitation of a noble Messenger” translates “innahu la-qawlu rasulin karim,” it is the speech transmitted by a noble messenger. Noble Messenger here is primarily Muhammad who recites the Reminder to the whole world, and via him Gabriel who is also noble, as with “recitation of a noble Messenger” earlier. Noble indicates honour, as with never-ending reward and outstanding character. Recitation, or “qawl,” is conveyed speech, not invented, as the well-preserved Record touched only by purified angels shows. This Quran, which would humble a mountain, which brings from darkness to light, which is Light in which belief is called, is noble because its carrier is noble and its source is Lord of all worlds.\n\nThis follows oaths by seen and unseen to affirm that what you see and do not see testifies that this is not ancient fables as the brute claimed because blessed with wealth and children, nor magic as those who said pure magic when foretold prophet came.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think “recitation of a noble Messenger” means the Messenger invented it. The next verses clarify it is revelation from the Lord of all worlds, and had he invented, he would be seized.\n\nFor believers, nobility of the Messenger invites trusting the recitation, listening and reasoning so as not to be among residents of Blaze who lament if only we had listened, and being among those who will be told to eat and drink joyfully for days gone by, whose elevated Garden fruit hangs within reach, because they accepted the noble recitation as noble and lived by it, walking upright and protecting families with it.",
  "41": "\"It is not the prose of a poet ˹as you claim˺, ˹yet˺ you hardly have any faith.\" defends the Quran after calling it recitation of a noble Messenger, negating a specific accusation.\n\n“It is not the prose of a poet as you claim, yet you hardly have any faith” translates “wa ma huwa bi-qawli sha‘ir qalilan ma tu’minun,” it is not the saying of a poet, little you believe. Poet here was a charge similar to ancient fables and pure magic, that the Quran, though it would humble a mountain, is merely poets’ rhymed speech that impresses like planks’ impressive speech. The Quran denies it, as it is not poet’s prose but Reminder, Light, Balance, and well-preserved Record that only purified touch, recited by a noble Messenger whose outstanding character and never-ending reward show sanity, not poetic frenzy.\n\n“yet you hardly have any faith” observes little faith, as with “you hardly give any thanks” after hearing, sight, intellect were given, and “you are hardly mindful” after fortune-teller denial next. Hardly having faith explains why poet charge is made; faith that guides hearts through adversity and that is with Allah in whom we believe and trust is minimal.\n\nThis follows seen and unseen oaths, showing that what you see, the flawless heavens and birds held, proves it is not poet’s invention.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think poetry itself is condemned. Poetry as art is not the issue; claiming revelation is mere poetry to dismiss it is.\n\nFor practice, move from hardly having faith to having faith that moves to protect families, to give rightful share, to walk upright. Let the Quran be read as recitation of a noble Messenger, not as poet’s prose, so your record becomes the happy “Read my record, I knew I would face reckoning” rather than the bitter “I wish I had not been given my record.”",
  "42": "\"Nor is it the mumbling of a fortune-teller, ˹yet˺ you are hardly mindful.\" negates the second accusation after poet, completing the pair of false labels for revelation.\n\n“Nor is it the mumbling of a fortune-teller, yet you are hardly mindful” translates “wa la bi-qawli kahin qalilan ma tadhakkarun,” nor the saying of a soothsayer, little you remember. Fortune-teller, or “kahin,” mumbles unseen claims, pretends to copy the unseen Record as in “Or do they have access to the unseen so they copy it?” The Quran denies it is such mumbling, as it is not unseen copying by a despicable oath-taker slanderer, but revelation from the Lord of all worlds descends between seven heavens, taught to an illiterate messenger who recites, purifies, teaches Book and wisdom, and whose pen writes truth.\n\n“yet you are hardly mindful” notes little remembrance, as with “you hardly give thanks” and “hardly have faith.” Hardly mindful indicates the Reminder for the whole world is heard but not retained, as attentive ears should grasp the Ark reminder but do not.\n\nThis follows poet denial, showing two dismissals, poetry for Beauty and fortune-telling for Unseen, both false, as with ancient fables and magic accusations when clear proofs came.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think fortune-telling is real unseen access. The Quran says devils’ eavesdropping is stoned by lamps as missiles, so mumbling is delusion.\n\nFor reflection, be mindful. Remember often after dispersing to seek bounty after Friday, remember whether speaking secretly or openly He knows best what is hidden, and remember the Inevitable Hour that the fortune-teller cannot predict but revelation informs. Mindfulness turns hardly mindful into attentive ear that grasps, saving from being among residents of Blaze who lament if only we had listened, and placing you among those told to eat joyfully for days gone by.\n\nHardly mindful also appears as hardly thankful and hardly faithful, a trio that the Quran pairs to show that faith, mindfulness, and thanks reinforce each other. When one is hardly, the others weaken, as with the garden owners who were hardly mindful of “Allah willing,” hardly faithful in withholding, and hardly thankful for provision. Mindfulness here means remembering that the Pen writes, that the well-preserved Record is guarded, and that the Inevitable will crush earth and mountains, so remembering now prevents the disgrace of eyes downcast when the Shin is bared.",
  "43": "\"˹It is˺ a revelation from the Lord of all worlds.\" affirms the true source after denying poet and fortune-teller. The verse is concise and foundational.\n\n“It is a revelation from the Lord of all worlds” translates “tanzilun min rabb al-‘alamin,” a sending down from the Lord, Cherisher, of all worlds, the One in Whose Hands rests all authority, the King, Holy, Almighty, who holds birds, smooths earth, adorns lamps, creates seven heavens in layers, and knows whatever you conceal and reveal. Revelation, or “tanzil,” is the same descending command between heavens, the Reminder, the Light, the Book and wisdom taught.\n\nFrom the Lord of all worlds indicates universal lordship, not tribal deity, as with “He is Allah, there is no god except Him, Knower of seen and unseen, Most Compassionate, Most Merciful” and with the cause to bring from darkness to light for the whole world. Worlds include heavens and earth that constantly glorify Him, and the Inevitable gathering of all.\n\nThis follows poet and fortune-teller denials, showing what it is not and what it is.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit revelation to one world, as ancient fables does by calling it old stories of one people.\n\nFor practice, receive it as revelation from the Lord of all worlds, not as poet or fortune-teller. Trust its informing that you will surely be resurrected then informed and that is easy for Allah, that the mutual loss and gain Day is coming, and that the right-hand’s bliss and left-hand’s pus are true, so you prepare by being among those given records in right hands who cry happily, by protecting families, and by walking upright, so revelation becomes your light ahead.\n\nRevelation from the Lord of all worlds also explains why it is reminder to those mindful and regret for disbelievers, absolute truth that makes faces gloomy for those who claimed it would never come. Lord of all worlds is the One who created Thamud and ‘Ad and seized them, who carried you in the Ark, who will blow the Trumpet, crush earth and mountains, tear sky, set angels on sides, bear Throne, and present you with no secret hidden. Revelation from Him therefore carries the same authority that holds birds, smooths earth, and adorned lamps. It is not poet’s capital or fortune-teller’s whisper that hardly has faith or mindfulness. For the living, receiving revelation means accepting its way of punishment in this world and far worse in Hereafter, its gardens of bliss for the righteous, its balance for justice, and its call to give rightful share, so revelation becomes your elevation, not your regret.",
  "44": "\"Had the Messenger made up something in Our Name.\" states the hypothetical that proves sincerity after affirming revelation from the Lord of all worlds. The verse introduces a severe conditional.\n\n“Had the Messenger made up something in Our Name” translates “wa law taqawwala ‘alayna ba‘da al-aqawil,” had he fabricated against Us any sayings. Taqawwala is to forge, to attribute invented speech as revelation, like claiming oaths bind until the Day whatever you decide or that scripture reads whatever you choose, but far graver, attributing lies to Allah, the worst wrong when invited to submit. The supposition is that the noble Messenger, proven not insane, of outstanding character with never-ending reward, had invented.\n\nThis hypothetical is not accusation, but proof by impossibility, showing how preserved the Record is, touched only by purified angels, and how the pen writes truthfully.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this suggests doubt about the Prophet. The condition is impossible for the outstanding, noble Messenger, used to show severity of punishment if it had occurred, thus proving it did not.\n\nFor reflection, the severity underscores how easily he could have been seized if he had invented, yet he was not, proving the Quran is indeed revelation, so ancient fables and poet charges collapse, and the Inevitable scenes, the Garden and Fire, are true, calling for listening and reasoning before being among residents of Blaze.\n\nThe severe conditional also shows how the Pen’s writing and the well-preserved Record are guarded. Had the Messenger made up something, the One who gradually draws rejecters in ways they cannot comprehend and whose planning is flawless would have stopped him immediately, as He stopped the garden while they slept and made Thamud’s blast overwhelming. The fact He did not stop the Messenger, but instead gave him never-ending reward, outstanding character, and support as Gabriel and righteous believers, proves the recitation is protected. For believers, this severity deters the same grave wrong, fabricating lies about Allah when invited to submit, which is the most wrongdoing that none should do. Instead, be among the submitted not wicked, whose scripture you read is the true Reminder, whose light is perfected, and whose Gardens are with their Lord, where fabrication is absent and only truth is recited joyfully.",
  "45": "\"We would have certainly seized him by his right hand.\" continues the conditional after “Had the Messenger made up something.” The verse details the requital.\n\n“We would have certainly seized him by his right hand” translates “la-akhadhna minhu bil-yamin,” We would have taken him by the right hand, or with power, as the crushing grip seized Pharaoh and the overturned, and as the garden was struck while they slept, and as the Inevitable will crush earth and mountains with one blow. Right hand indicates strength and authority, as with the kingdom in His Hands and honour belonging to Allah. Seizing by the right hand is to overpower and halt, opposite to being carried in the Ark and held as birds are held.\n\nThis follows the hypothetical fabrication to show that even the most beloved, the noble Messenger of outstanding character, would not be exempt if he invented, proving the well-preserved Record’s protection and the pen’s fidelity.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to imagine Allah has a hand like creation. The Quran affirms without likening, as with Shin bared and establishment on the Throne.\n\nFor the audience, the firmness assures that revelations about the Inevitable, about Thamud’s blast, ‘Ad’s wind, the Ark, the Trumpet, and the two foods are not invented, since the Messenger was not seized, but instead is supported as Gabriel, righteous believers, and angels are supporters, and his Lord chose Jonah to be righteous after grace. Therefore, trust the revelation and prepare, so seizure is not your fate as with the left-hand who will be told “Seize and shackle them.”\n\nSeizing by the right hand also signifies the end of plotting to extinguish light with mouths and to compromise for yield. Right hand is strength, as with honour belonging to Allah and the Powerless force that would help instead of the Most Compassionate. Seizing by it is humbling, as with the snout marked on the brute with wealth and children and as with the horror cast on fortified hearts. The One who merges night into day and knows what is hidden in the heart can seize any plotter, as He seized the garden, ‘Ad’s wind, and Pharaoh’s grip. The hypothetical seizing assures that what is with Allah is better than amusement and merchandise and that the Best Provider will not let fabrication stand. To avoid seizing, cling to the firm rope, be true to trusts, and deliver the Reminder to the whole world with outstanding character, so your right hand receives its record happily and says “Read my record, I surely knew I would face my reckoning” in a life of bliss, not seized.",
  "46": "\"then severed his aorta.\" continues the severe conditional after seizing by the right hand. The image is graphic to show seriousness of inventing revelation.\n\n“then severed his aorta” translates “thumma la-qata‘na minhu al-watin,” then We would have cut his aorta, the great vessel that sustains life, the lifeline whose severing ends life instantly. Aorta, or “watin,” is the artery that carries blood from the heart, so cutting it is the most decisive ending, as with the crushing grip that seized Pharaoh, the blast for Thamud, and the ashes for the garden. The One who holds all authority and created death and life to test can end any life with a single blow, and would end even the most beloved’s life if he had attributed falsehood to the Lord of all worlds.\n\nThis follows seizing by the right hand to show sequential enforcement. Seizing stops, severing ends, leaving no shield, as next verse says none could shield him from Us.\n\nThis hypothetical proves that the Quran is not poet’s prose, not fortune-teller’s mumbling, not ancient fables, but revelation, because the Messenger was not seized or cut, but honoured with never-ending reward and outstanding character, chosen and made righteous like Jonah after grace, and supported as Gabriel, righteous believers, and angels support him.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as harshness toward the Prophet. It is honour by protection; the severity shows how seriously Allah guards the well-preserved Record touched only by purified angels and the pen’s truthful writing, ensuring that what you see and do not see in the oath remains pure.\n\nFor believers, the severing image deters inventing in Allah’s Name, whether claiming oaths bind until the Day whatever you decide, claiming scripture reads whatever you choose, or claiming associate-gods will intercede. Instead, hold to the revelation from the Lord of all worlds that is absolute truth and reminder to those mindful, so your aorta remains by grace and your record is read happily, not severed.",
  "47": "\"and none of you could have shielded him ˹from Us˺!\" concludes the conditional that began with “Had the Messenger made up something.” The verse states that no intercession could prevent requital.\n\n“and none of you could have shielded him from Us!” translates “fa-ma minkum min ahadin ‘anhu hajizin,” none among you could be a barrier, a defender, against Us. Hajizin is one who holds back, shields, as with false oaths as shields that hindered, with worthless planks leaned that thought every cry against them, and with honour claimed to expel inferior. No one, not hypocrites who promised to leave and help, not the brute with wealth and children, not the slanderer or withholder, and not even the most righteous believers, could shield the Messenger if he had invented, because the Kingdom is His and all praise is for Him and He is Most Capable, and honour belongs to Allah.\n\nThis follows seizing and severing to show that the hypothetical punishment would be unavoidable, just as the Inevitable Hour is unavoidable, as the earth and mountains crushed with one blow is unavoidable, and as the Fire’s roaring for its residents is unavoidable.\n\nThe exclamation with “from Us!” emphasizes source, the Lord of all worlds whose Hands hold authority, whose command descends between seven heavens, and whose threat the deniers asked when it would come, knowing that is with Allah alone.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this isolates the Prophet as unprotected. It shows the opposite, that his protection is precisely because he did not invent, so he is guarded, his light is perfected, and his pen writes truth for the whole world.\n\nFor the community, the impossibility of shielding teaches reliance. Do not rely on a powerless force to help instead of the Most Compassionate, on merchandise to provide if He withholds, on associate-gods to bring, or on oaths to bind. Rely on sincere faith in the Greatest and encouraging feeding of the poor, so the only shielding you need is forgiveness and mighty reward for those in awe without seeing, which the Most Compassionate grants, and no chain of seventy arms need be feared.",
  "48": "\"Indeed, this ˹Quran˺ is a reminder to those mindful ˹of Allah˺.\" affirms the Quran’s function after the severe hypothetical that proved its preservation. The verse defines its audience.\n\n“Indeed, this Quran is a reminder to those mindful of Allah” translates “innahu la-tadhkiratun lil-muttaqin,” it is reminder for the God-conscious. Mindful, or “muttaqin,” are the righteous who will have Gardens of Bliss with their Lord, those in awe without seeing who have forgiveness and mighty reward, those who protect families from Fire, who are mindful to best ability, hear and obey, spend, and are saved from selfishness. Reminder, or “tadhkirah,” is the Ark carried as reminder for attentive ears, the mountain-humbling Reminder, and the Reminder to the whole world that disbelievers tried to cut down with eyes.\n\nThis follows seizing and severing to show what the preserved Quran is for. It is not poet’s prose, not fortune-teller’s mumbling, but reminder, as with the reminder that the Inevitable Hour is coming, that the garden owners needed “Allah willing,” and that wealth and children are only a test.\n\nA reminder to the mindful indicates that mindfulness makes the reminder beneficial, as smoothed earth benefits the walker who remembers that to Him is resurrection, and as flawless heavens benefit the looker who looks again. The same Quran is recited to those who deny and say Allah revealed nothing, but they are not mindful, so they are extremely astray and will be among residents of Blaze who lament if only we had listened.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think reminder is only for the already perfect. Mindful includes those who falter, whose hearts have certainly faltered yet turn in sincere repentance so light is perfected.\n\nFor practice, become mindful to benefit from reminder. Guard chastity like Mary, pray like Pharaoh’s wife for a house near Allah, make room, lend good loans, and walk upright, so reminder becomes your light ahead and to the right, your elevated Garden with near fruit, and your joyful eating for days gone by, rather than the oozing pus that only evildoers eat.",
  "49": "\"And We certainly know that some of you will persist in denial.\" acknowledges reality after defining the Quran as reminder to the mindful. The verse shows divine knowledge includes persistence.\n\n“And We certainly know that some of you will persist in denial” translates “wa inna la-na‘lamu anna minkum mukadhdhibin,” We know among you are deniers who will continue to say ancient fables, pure magic, poet, fortune-teller, and extremely astray. Knowledge here is the Knower of seen and unseen, Most Subtle, All-Aware who knows whether you speak secretly or openly and what is hidden in the heart, who knows best who has strayed and who is rightly guided, and who keeps account while they forgot.\n\nSome, or “minkum,” among you indicates that even within the audience that hears the Reminder for the whole world, some will persist, as with Thamud and ‘Ad denied the Striking, Pharaoh and overturned indulged, and garden owners withheld until deprived. Persistence is not a single doubt, but continuing after clear proofs, Balance, and Light.\n\nThis follows reminder to the mindful, showing two responses, mindfulness and persistent denial, whose destinations are Garden of Bliss and raging Flame, submitted vs wicked, right hand vs left hand.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use this as excuse to label others. “Some of you” is known to Allah, the All-Aware of what you do, not to humans to declare finally.\n\nFor believers, knowing some will persist should not cause despair or compromise. Do not give in to deniers, do not obey the despicable oath-taker, slanderer, withholder, and be patient with your Lord’s decree, not like Jonah in distress. Continue delivering the Reminder with outstanding character, trusting that the pen writes, and that the Inevitable will sort, so your persistence is in listening and reasoning, not in denial, and you will be among those who confess “We have been wrongdoers” early and are given better, not among those who persist until regret.",
  "50": "\"and it will surely be a source of regret for the disbelievers.\" foretells the feeling that follows persistence in denial. The verse links the Quran to future remorse.\n\n“and it will surely be a source of regret for the disbelievers” translates “wa innahu la-hasratun ‘ala al-kafirin,” it will be anguish, sorrow, upon the disbelievers, when they see the torment drawing near with gloomy faces, when they hear roaring as it boils over, when they taste total loss and are told away with residents of Blaze, and when they hear “This is what you claimed would never come.” Regret, or “hasrah,” is deep sorrow for missed chance, as with “if only we had listened and reasoned” and “I wish I had not been given my record, nor known my reckoning, would that death was the end,” and wealth and authority stripped, and no close friend, only pus.\n\nThis follows “some will persist in denial,” showing that persistence ends in regret, as with garden owners who proceeded fixated then said “We have been deprived” and hypocrites whose oaths as shields became severe, humiliating punishment.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think regret is merely emotional. Regret here is recognition that the Reminder to the mindful, the absolute truth, was ignored.\n\nFor the living, avoid hasrah by heeding reminder now. Be in awe without seeing, whose mighty reward is forgiveness, be mindful to best ability, hear and obey, spend, protect families, and walk upright, so the Quran is not regret upon you but reminder for you, and its recitation by the noble Messenger leads to “Read my record” joy, not to hasrah that only evildoers taste as pus.\n\nRegret for disbelievers also follows persistence after reminder to the mindful. Those who persist after the mountain would be humbled, after the Pen swears, and after the garden’s ashes, persist as Thamud and ‘Ad denied the Striking and as Pharaoh indulged. Regret, or “hasrah,” is the hasrah of the garden owners who said “We have been deprived” after “We lost our way,” and of the Fire’s residents who say “if only we had listened.” The Quran will be hasrah on the Day the Inevitable has come to pass, when the sky is torn frail and the Throne is borne, and none of you could have shielded fabrication. To avoid this hasrah, be among the mindful now, in awe without seeing, whose mighty reward is forgiveness, who move about regions and eat provision knowing to Him is resurrection, and who turn in sincere repentance so light is perfected. Then the Quran is not hasrah but Tadhkirah, reminder that leads to the elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach and you are told to eat joyfully.",
  "51": "\"And indeed, this ˹Quran˺ is the absolute truth.\" seals the surah’s argument after regret, affirming the Reminder’s status. The verse uses the highest certainty.\n\n“And indeed, this Quran is the absolute truth” translates “wa innahu la-haqqu al-yaqin,” it is the truth of certainty, the reality whose certainty is absolute, as with “The Inevitable Hour, What is the Inevitable Hour? What will make you realize what it is?” The absolute truth is the certainty that Thamud’s blast, ‘Ad’s wind, Pharaoh’s grip, the Ark, the Trumpet’s one blast, crushing, torn sky, Throne borne, and presentation with no secrets hidden are true, as is the Garden’s elevated life of bliss and the Fire’s chain of seventy arms.\n\nHaqq al-yaqin is stronger than knowledge of certainty or eye of certainty; it is truth experienced, as with tasting evil consequences and then being told “This is what you claimed would never come.” The Quran is that truth in words, the same truth that would humble a mountain, that is Light, that is well-preserved Record touched only by purified.\n\nThis follows regret, showing regret is for denying absolute truth, not opinion.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat absolute truth as mere claim. The Quran supports it with flawless heavens you can look again at, birds held, earth smoothed, and histories that left none alive.\n\nFor practice, treat the Quran as absolute truth now, not after regret. That means not equating submitted and wicked, not having scripture that reads whatever you choose, not having oaths binding until the Day, not having associate-gods to bring, but having the Reminder for the whole world, walking upright, and being among the righteous who will have Gardens of Bliss, whose absolute truth will be eating and drinking joyfully for days gone by, while others face the absolute truth of pus that only evildoers eat.",
  "52": "\"So glorify the Name of your Lord, the Greatest.\" closes Surah al-Haqqah as it closed Surah al-Waqi‘ah, with glorification of the Greatest. The command is the response to absolute truth.\n\n“So glorify the Name of your Lord, the Greatest” translates “fa-sabbih bi-smi rabbika al-‘azim,” glorify the Name of your Lord, the Greatest, free Him from imperfection, praise Him. Greatest, or “‘Azim,” is the One in Whose Hands rests all authority, Most Capable of everything, Knower of seen and unseen, Most Subtle, All-Aware, who created seven heavens in layers, who holds birds, who is King, Holy, Perfect, Source of Serenity, Watcher, Almighty, Majestic, Creator, Inventor, Shaper with Most Beautiful Names. Glorifying His Name includes the pen’s writing, the Throne borne, and the reminder that brings from darkness.\n\nSo indicates therefore, because this Quran is absolute truth, reminder to the mindful, revelation from the Lord of all worlds, recitation of a noble Messenger not poet or fortune-teller, witnessed by seen and unseen, and because the Inevitable will have come to pass with Trumpet, crushing, and presentation, therefore glorify.\n\nThis follows absolute truth, as sincere repentance before that truth perfects light. Glorifying here is not mere phrase, but aligning life, as with being mindful to best ability, hearing and obeying, spending, protecting families, and walking upright.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to reduce glorifying to tongue without deeds. The Quran pairs glorifying with best deeds test, where outstanding character is best, and where hearing, sight, intellect hardly thanked become thanks through glorification.\n\nFor daily life, glorify the Greatest now, as the mountain would be humbled, as the angels on sides glorify around the torn sky, so that when you are presented and none of your secrets stay hidden, your record reads with glorification, your life is bliss in elevated Garden, and your greeting is “Eat and drink joyfully,” and the Greatest who created death and life to test which is best in deeds declares you among the righteous whose Gardens of Bliss are with their Lord."
};
